│    ├─26 text " lists, for\nevery linked language, "
│    ├─27 inlineCode "reads"
│    └─28 text " or the reason."
├─6  paragraph[7]
│    ├─0 text "A reader gets the rows of its own relation to write and no other: "
│    ├─1 inlineCode "Reader<R>"
│    ├─2 text " is given "
│    ├─3 inlineCode "&mut dyn Rows<R's row>"
│    ├─4 text ",\n"
│    ├─5 inlineCode "ManifestReader<R>"
│    └─6 text " the same. The engine reads the tree once for the readers of every wanted relation, so a\nlanguage never receives the writer of the whole store."
├─7  heading[1]
│    │ depth: 2
│    └─0 text "Scopes"
├─8  paragraph[5]
│    ├─0 text "Every scope has a row of "
│    ├─1 inlineCode "scope"
│    ├─2 text ", including the top-level ones: a function, a class or any other item that makes a scope at the top of a file has the file's module scope for its parent. The module scope itself has a row too, with its own node for its parent (the root of a tree is its own parent in the element table, and here too), so the shape has no parentless row: a scope with no parent is not a shape. It is the only row of its file with that. The conformance holds it: of the scope rows of each sample file, exactly one has the same node for parent and child, and every other has a parent that is a child of a row of the file. A file with nothing in it still gives that one row. Rust's scopes are the module of the file, a nested module, an "
│    ├─3 inlineCode "impl"
│    └─4 text ", a trait and a function."
├─9  heading[1]
│    │ depth: 2
│    └─0 text "Documents"
├─10 paragraph[13]
│    ├─0  text "A language of documents (Markdown, HTML) says what a document declares with the shared kinds of "
│    ├─1  inlineCode "defines"
│    ├─2  text ": a titled section is "
│    ├─3  inlineCode "defines { kind: section }"
│    ├─4  text ", nested through "
│    ├─5  inlineCode "scope"
│    ├─6  text " as functions nest in classes, and an anchor, an id or a label is "
│    ├─7  inlineCode "defines { kind: label }"
│    ├─8  text ". Code languages never write these two kinds. Markdown reads them: a heading is a section (named by its title, held by the section around it, scoped from the heading to the next heading as shallow or shallower), and the definition of a link reference, of a footnote and an "
│    ├─9  inlineCode "id"
│    ├─10 text " or "
│    ├─11 inlineCode "a name"
│    └─12 text " of raw HTML are labels."
├─11 paragraph[25]
│    ├─0  text "A link target is "
│    ├─1  inlineCode "reference.target"
│    ├─2  text ", and the language resolves it to a run-relative path as it resolves an import ("
│    ├─3  inlineCode "static_import.target"
│    ├─4  text "): "
│    ├─5  inlineCode "[text](../guide.md#setup)"
│    ├─6  text " in "
│    ├─7  inlineCode "docs/a.md"
│    ├─8  text " has the target "
│    ├─9  inlineCode "guide.md#setup"
│    ├─10 text ", where "
│    ├─11 inlineCode "docs/"
│    ├─12 text " is the document's own directory; a fragment alone is the document's own path with it ("
│    ├─13 inlineCode "docs/a.md#top"
│    ├─14 text "), a path from the root ("
│    ├─15 inlineCode "/x.md"
│    ├─16 text ") is the run's own "
│    ├─17 inlineCode "x.md"
│    ├─18 text ", and a destination with a scheme stays as written. The language does it where it builds the tree: "
│    ├─19 inlineCode "read_tree"
│    ├─20 text " is given the request with the file's path, and writes the resolved target as the decoded text of the first marker of the link, which "
│    ├─21 inlineCode "named_by"
│    ├─22 text " reads back (Markdown's adapter shows how). RyoQL has nothing for it: a rule compares the target with a path or a "
│    ├─23 inlineCode "defines.name"
│    └─24 text " as it compares any two names."
├─12 paragraph[9]
│    ├─0 text "The text a reader reads is "
│    ├─1 inlineCode "prose { id, owner, text, file, span }"
│    ├─2 text ": one row for each run of a document: a paragraph, the title of a heading, a cell of a table, the text an item of a list holds itself (an item of paragraphs gives the rows of its paragraphs). The "
│    ├─3 inlineCode "text"
│    ├─4 text " joins the markup of the run and masks what a reader does not read as prose: inline code, math and raw HTML become a space, a break is a space, and the white space between words is one space. The "
│    ├─5 inlineCode "owner"
│    ├─6 text " is the section that holds the run, or the document. A language that is not a document answers "
│    ├─7 inlineCode "NotADocument"
│    └─8 text "."
├─13 heading[1]
│    │ depth: 2
│    └─0 text "Directives"
├─14 paragraph[21]
│    ├─0  text "An instruction to the build tool that a file carries is "
│    ├─1  inlineCode "directive { file, span, kind, text }"
│    ├─2  text ": "
│    ├─3  inlineCode "kind"
│    ├─4  text " is the directive's name as the language writes it and "
│    ├─5  inlineCode "text"
│    ├─6  text " its argument text as the tool reads it. Go's "
│    ├─7  inlineCode "//go:build linux && amd64"
│    ├─8  text " is "
│    ├─9  inlineCode "kind: go:build, text: linux && amd64"
│    ├─10 text " and a header "
│    ├─11 inlineCode "// +build linux"
│    ├─12 text " is "
│    ├─13 inlineCode "kind: +build, text: linux"
│    ├─14 text "; the line is also a "
│    ├─15 inlineCode "comment"
│    ├─16 text " row, and it is no "
│    ├─17 inlineCode "prose"
│    ├─18 text " row, which is the text a reader of a document reads. The kinds are the language's words and ryo keeps no list of them. A language with no such instruction answers "
│    ├─19 inlineCode "NoSuchConstruct"
│    └─20 text "."
