│    └─0 text "The conformance holds each reason against the class of the relation and against the other answers of the language."
├─18 heading[1]
│    │ depth: 2
│    └─0 text "Manifests are files"
├─19 paragraph[15]
│    ├─0  text "A column that names a manifest holds the kind File, never text: "
│    ├─1  inlineCode "workspace_member.workspace"
│    ├─2  text " and "
│    ├─3  inlineCode "package"
│    ├─4  text ", "
│    ├─5  inlineCode "file_package.package"
│    ├─6  text ", "
│    ├─7  inlineCode "package_dependency.source"
│    ├─8  text " and "
│    ├─9  inlineCode "target"
│    ├─10 text ". A File is the number of the file among the files the run lists, in the order of their paths, so a rule joins a manifest with "
│    ├─11 inlineCode "filesystem.file.path"
│    ├─12 text " as it joins any file. A manifest the run does not list (a workspace names a member the scope leaves out) is still a file the rows name: its number is the one after the last file the run lists, in the order of the paths of such manifests, and the language says which they are in "
│    ├─13 inlineCode "Resolved::unlisted"
│    └─14 text ". Two languages that both name manifests the run does not list are refused: the numbers after the listed files are one language's to give."
├─20 heading[1]
│    │ depth: 3
│    └─0 text "What each language's manifests say"
├─21 paragraph[25]
│    ├─0  text "A manifest is a file the language names ("
│    ├─1  inlineCode "manifests()"
│    ├─2  text ", the last part of the path) and reads with the parser of its format. Every language answers the same relations from it: a "
│    ├─3  emphasis[1]
│    │    └─0 text "package"
│    ├─4  text " is a manifest that names one; a "
│    ├─5  emphasis[1]
│    │    └─0 text "workspace member"
│    ├─6  text " is a package the workspace manifest holds; a "
│    ├─7  emphasis[1]
│    │    └─0 text "dependency"
│    ├─8  text " is a requirement of a package on another package "
│    ├─9  strong[1]
│    │    └─0 text "of the repository"
│    ├─10 text ", written with the kind below. The rows name manifests as Files and never text, so a requirement on a package outside the repository (a package of PyPI, a module of the Go proxy, a package of npm) is not a row of "
│    ├─11 inlineCode "package_dependency"
│    ├─12 text ": that relation is the graph of the repository's own packages. The "
│    ├─13 inlineCode "target"
│    ├─14 text " of a requirement is the manifest of the package of the repository the requirement names, found by the name the format normalizes; a requirement none of the repository's packages answers to has no row. "
│    ├─15 inlineCode "package_dependency.package"
│    ├─16 text " is the name as the format normalizes it and "
│    ├─17 inlineCode "binding"
│    ├─18 text " the name as the manifest writes it; "
│    ├─19 inlineCode "condition"
│    ├─20 text " is the condition text as written (a marker, a build tag); "
│    ├─21 inlineCode "optional"
│    ├─22 text " is "
│    ├─23 inlineCode "Optional"
│    └─24 text " for what the package needs only when asked for."
