├─57 heading[1]
│    │ depth: 2
│    └─0 text "A file is read, never refused"
├─58 paragraph[7]
│    ├─0 text "Bytes that are not valid text are read: the valid text keeps its tree, and the rest is an error token with a diagnostic, at the offsets it has in the file (the Rust language reads each byte that is part of no character as a control character the lexer reports). "
│    ├─1 inlineCode "ParseError"
│    ├─2 text " has no case for them: a refusal is for a language that does not read the file ("
│    ├─3 inlineCode "Unsupported"
│    ├─4 text ") and for a library that fails ("
│    ├─5 inlineCode "Library"
│    └─6 text "), and the shared rails treat a refusal of a file of the suite or the corpus as a failure."
├─59 heading[1]
│    │ depth: 2
│    └─0 text "What a language works out of a file once"
├─60 paragraph[17]
│    ├─0  text "A language that would search a file again for each element a reader is given (TOML: the header an entry belongs to, the ordinal of an entry) works it out once, when it parses the file, and its readers read it. "
│    ├─1  inlineCode "Parse::FileModel"
│    ├─2  text " is the type ("
│    ├─3  inlineCode "()"
│    ├─4  text " for a language that needs none; it is "
│    ├─5  inlineCode "Default"
│    ├─6  text ") and "
│    ├─7  inlineCode "Parse::file_model(tree, model)"
│    ├─8  text " refills it: the engine makes one model for each worker, keeps it with the worker's table between files and gives it back, so a file costs no allocation the last did not pay, and the language clears what it keeps and writes the file's. The model and the language that makes it may be private to their crate: the associated type of a crate-private type is crate-private, and the engine holds the model behind an interface that never names it ("
│    ├─9  inlineCode "tests/ui/pass_crate_private_file_model.rs"
│    ├─10 text "); a reader is declared "
│    ├─11 inlineCode "Item => Relation, model(FileModel) |tree, model, at, symbols| { .. }"
│    ├─12 text ". The text a library decodes from a token (the value of a string with its escapes resolved) is written by the adapter with "
│    ├─13 inlineCode "decoded_token"
│    ├─14 text ", and a reader reads it with "
│    ├─15 inlineCode "decoded_at"
│    └─16 text "; no language decodes text a second time."
